   * persistence program; docs/designissues/2026-07-06-inmemory-bytes-
   * store.md). Thin fn-shape wrappers over Factoidal.toCottas/
   * openCottas/queryCottas/closeCottas (npm/factoidal/browser.js,
   * already exported there) -- no fn wrapper existed until post 42
   * called one, same masked-fn-surface class as the rhoDf-family and
   * sigmoid gaps above (the node test harness can't see a missing browser-
   * layer wrapper; only a live cell on a real post exposes it). This
   * is the browser tier of the same reader/writer the CLI's
   * `--data-cottas`/`--data-cottas-mem` serves on disk
   * (skills/disk-storage-format/SKILL.md §6): bytes held in the wasm
   * heap instead of a file, read-only, no entailment, no write
   * overlay -- see Factoidal.queryCottas's own doc comment in
   * browser.js for the full divergence list from fn.query().
   */
  async toCottas(dataset) { return Factoidal.toCottas(dataset.toNQuads()); },
  async openCottas(bytes) { return Factoidal.openCottas(bytes); },
  async queryCottas(handle, sparql) {
    if (typeof handle !== "string") {
      throw new TypeError("queryCottas: handle must be the string openCottas() returned");
    }
    const r = await Factoidal.queryCottas(handle, sparql);
    if (r.kind === "ask") return !!r.boolean;
    if (r.kind === "construct") return new FnDataset(r.nquads);
    const rows = (r.srj.results && r.srj.results.bindings) || [];
    return rows.map((row) => {
      const map = new Map();
      for (const [name, term] of Object.entries(row)) map.set(name, srjTermToFn(term));
      return map;
    });
  },
  async closeCottas(handle) { return Factoidal.closeCottas(handle); },
  /**
   * SPARQL 1.1 §17.6 extension functions (issue #463) + SERVICE
   * endpoint snapshots (issue #57 family) — thin pass-throughs to the
   * browser adapter, which owns the registry + async trampoline.
   */
  async registerExtensionFunction(iri, f) { return Factoidal.registerExtensionFunction(iri, f); },
  async unregisterExtensionFunction(iri) { return Factoidal.unregisterExtensionFunction(iri); },
  async clearExtensionFunctions() { return Factoidal.clearExtensionFunctions(); },
  async registerServiceEndpoint(iri, data, options) { return Factoidal.registerServiceEndpoint(iri, data, options); },
  async clearServiceEndpoints() { return Factoidal.clearServiceEndpoints(); },
  /**
   * Load the vendored wikifn-fstar compiled Wikifunctions engine
   * (hub post 35; provenance in web/hub/assets/wikifn/README.md).
   * Resolves once globalThis.wikifnCompiledCall / wikifnEngineCall
   * exist; idempotent. The node-side twin lives in
   * tests/hub/post35_test.mjs (reads the same vendored file from
   * disk), mirroring post24's fn.queryHdt browser-vs-test duality.
   */
  async loadWikifunctions() {
    if (typeof globalThis.wikifnCompiledCall === "function") return true;
    await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      const s = document.createElement("script");
      s.src = "../assets/wikifn/wikifn_engine.js";
      s.onload = resolve;
      s.onerror = () => reject(new Error("could not load wikifn_engine.js"));
      document.head.appendChild(s);
    });
    if (typeof globalThis.wikifnCompiledCall !== "function") {
      throw new Error("wikifn_engine.js loaded but did not export wikifnCompiledCall");
    }
    return true;
  },
  /**
   * Load the Lean 4 engine compiled to WebAssembly (hub post 36).
   * A SECOND, independent engine on the page: the rest of `fn` is the
   * F*-derived OCaml compiled by js_of_ocaml, while this is Lean source
   * compiled Lean -> C -> wasm32 by Emscripten. Built by
   * formal/lean4/Wasm/build-wasm.sh; artifacts in web/hub/assets/l4/.
   *
   * Idempotent: the promise is cached, so a page with several L4 cells
   * instantiates one module. The node-side twin lives in
   * tests/hub/post36_test.mjs (imports the same loader from disk),
   * mirroring the fn.loadWikifunctions duality above.
   */
  async loadL4() {
    if (!this._l4Promise) {
      // `?v=` is the eleventy build's BUILD_VERSION (docs/.eleventy.js
      // computes it once and exposes it as the `buildVersion` template
      // global — the same value docs/sw.js's cache name is stamped
      // with). Changing the import URL per build is the fix for issue
      // #584: the ServiceWorker serves same-origin assets stale-while-
      // revalidate, so without a version-busted URL the first page
      // load after a deploy can pair the new hub HTML with the
      // PREVIOUS build's l4factoidal.js.
      this._l4Promise = import("../assets/l4/l4factoidal.js?v={{ buildVersion }}").then((m) => m.loadL4());
    }
    return this._l4Promise;
  },
  /** The Lean-side ABI version string (hub post 36). */
  async l4Version() {
    return (await this.loadL4()).version();
  },
  /**
   * Evaluate a Basic Graph Pattern with Lean's
   * `L4Factoidal.SPARQL.evalBgp` inside wasm (hub post 36). `data` and
   * `bgp` are arrays of SPARQL-Results-JSON-shaped term objects; the
   * result is a SPARQL 1.1 Query Results JSON document.
   */
  async l4BgpQuery(data, bgp) {
    return (await this.loadL4()).bgpQuery(data, bgp);
  },
  /**
   * Generic dispatch into the Lean 4 engine (hub post 38): `op` is an
   * op name from formal/lean4/Wasm/Dispatch.lean ("ops" lists them),
   * `args` an array of positional string arguments. Returns the parsed
   * {"ok":true,...} envelope; throws when the Lean side reports
   * {"ok":false,"error":...}. One wrapper serves every dispatch op,
   * because the envelopes match bin/npm-entry/entry_jsoo.ml op for op.
   */
  async l4Call(op, args) {
    return (await this.loadL4()).call(op, args);
  },
  /**
   * Parse an RDF document into a Lean-side dataset handle (hub posts
   * 36/38; issue #585's page-level half: WHY ARE WE PARSING TTL TO
   * JSON NOT A GRAPH API -- owner, 2026-08-25). Mirrors `fn.parse`'s
   * contract (`text, {format, baseIRI} -> Dataset`) but backs the
   * result with `datasetOpen` (`Wasm/Ops/Handles.lean`): the text
   * crosses the JS/wasm boundary exactly once, here, and every later
   * `fn.l4Query`/`fn.l4Update` call carries only the returned
   * L4Dataset's handle.
   */
  async l4Parse(text, options) {
    const opts = options || {};
    const engine = await this.loadL4();
    const r = engine.call("datasetOpen", [text, opts.format || "turtle", opts.baseIRI || ""]);
